From 2031a20d5b322d14446d2a64cef20be48b08461e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 02:43:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 377/377] parse/expand: `$crate` at the head of an attribute
 macro path

Found by the attr-path-crate-pm-test kit while fixing gix-protocol's `#[crate::bisync::only_async]`: the same
attribute written through a macro_rules body, `#[$crate::bisync::only_async] $($t)*`, failed to parse,

    MACRO<::"dollar"::drop_async> error:0: Unexpected token TOK_STRING"dollar"/**/, expected TOK_IDENT

The macro_rules evaluator spells `$crate` as `::"<crate name>"::` (a string token after `::`), for the defining
crate too: its `crate`-keyword branch never runs because the evaluator is always given a crate name. Parse_Path
reads that spelling back; Parse_MetaItem read the leading `::` and then wanted an identifier, so an attribute
macro named through `$crate` (a facade crate's convenience macro wrapping its re-exported proc macro, the
gix-protocol `mod bisync` shape reached from another crate) could not be used from any macro_rules.

AST::AttributeName gets `crate_name`: Parse_MetaItem fills it from `::"name"::` and from an interpolated
absolute path to a named crate (`#[$p]` with `$p:path` holding another crate's `$crate`), Expand_LookupMacro
starts the macro lookup at that crate's root (this crate's own name resolves to the local root, as for paths),
and the three places that spell an attribute name print it back: the debug/DumpAST printer as `::"name"::`
(what the lexer accepts), stringify!'s `$m:meta` the same, the proc-macro bridge's forwarded attributes as the
one `$crate` ident visit_path sends (send_dollar_crate). ast/attrs.hpp changes: a wide rebuild, no rlib format
change.

Assisted-By: Claude Fable 5.1
